{##
 # Standardised Test
 #
 # @param array     externalUser
 # @param int|null  year
 #}
{% include '@templates/Avatar.twig' %}

{% include '@templates/Navigation.twig' with {
    elements: navigation
} %}

{% include '@templates/Navigation.twig' with {
    elements: subnavigation
} %}

<script type="text/javascript" src="/javascript/components/highcharts-release/highcharts.js"></script>

<div class="achievement-report standardised-test">
    <div class="row">
        {% if literacy.visible %}
        <div class="small-6 columns">
            {% include '@standardisedTest/LiteracyNumeracy/Literacy/graph.twig' with { literacy: literacy } %}
        </div>
        {% endif %}
    </div>

    <div class="row">
        <div class="small-12 island">
            <section class="content">
                <p>
                    This literacy and numeracy progress report shows you where your daughter is achieving in relation
                    to the Diocesan expectations for her age. The shaded area on the graph shows the Diocesan
                    Expectations based on student performance in assessments. These expectations make explicit the
                    standard of reading, writing and numeracy expertise that students are expected to reach. They are
                    high but attainable standards based on consistent expectations for learning.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The stars indicate the level of achievement your daughter has reached. This is based on an overall
                    teacher judgement. Making an overall judgement means drawing on a range of evidence gathered and
                    analysed to make an informed decision on the student's performance. Data from e-asTTle is one of
                    the measures used to inform the overall teacher judgement along with classroom based assessment.
                    Aligned to the New Zealand curriculum, e-asTTle is an online tool to assess a student's overall
                    achievement in reading, writing and mathematics, and is norm referenced to a sample of other
                    students in New Zealand.
                </p>
            </section>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
